To the young gaping heir his father's fate.PROGNOS'TICATED, pp. Foreshown; foretold.PROGNOS'TICATING, ppr. Foreshowing; foretelling.PROGNOSTICA'TION, n. The act of foreshowing a future event by present signs.

1. The act of foretelling an event by present signs.

2. A foretoken; previous sign.PROGNOS'TICATOR, n. A foreknower or foreteller of a future event by present signs.PROLONG', v.t. L.pro and longus. See Long.

1. To lengthen in time; to extend the duration of. Temperate habits tend to prolong life.

2. To lengthen; to draw out in time by delay; to continue.

